  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SPVO &#x2013; Empowering smallholder women in shea production in Northern Ghana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Women shea collectors in West Africa are essential to the global shea industry, yet most earn too little to sustain a decent standard of living. Key challenges include lack of market access, low shea nut prices, low and variable yields, and limited access to alternative income sources. Due to urgent cash needs, around 20% of women sell their shea nuts at extremely low prices, which undermines their ability to meet longer-term financial needs.To strengthen the livelihoods of shea collectors, Sommalife Impact Group B.V., Vitara Ltd., and Solidaridad West Africa have partnered under the Female Aggregating Association (FAA) initiative. This initiative aims to empower women to form structured, environmentally responsible associations that actively participate in the value chain; from collection to market access.FAAs focus on three strategic areas. First, they serve as local aggregation hubs, connecting collectors from surrounding communities to premium markets while also providing access to pre-financing. Second, FAAs lead community-based conservation and reforestation efforts, restoring degraded parklands with shea, baobab, and other native tree species. Third, they promote social well-being through child protection committees linked to local social welfare authorities, as well as gender equality programs using the Gender Action and Learning System (GALS). Finally, to reduce dependency on shea and increase financial resilience, the initiative supports the establishment of Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLAs) and provides training in income diversification.</narrative>
  </description>
